[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"Microservices Design Patterns & Decomposition Strategies"},{"order":2,"type":"subheading","text":"Introduction to Microservices Architecture"},{"order":3,"type":"paragraph","text":"Transitioning from a monolithic architecture to microservices requires careful planning, robust decomposition strategies, and an understanding of core design patterns. Microservices structure an application as a collection of loosely coupled, independently deployable services organized around business capabilities."},{"order":4,"type":"subheading","text":"Decomposition Strategies"},{"order":5,"type":"paragraph","text":"Breaking down a monolith is not trivial. Two primary strategies dictate how you split your domain:"},{"order":6,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Decompose by Business Capability: Define services corresponding to business capabilities (e.g., Ordering, Billing, Shipping)."},{"order":7,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Decompose by Subdomain: Use Domain-Driven Design (DDD) subdomains (Core, Supporting, Generic) to isolate complex business logic."},{"order":8,"type":"subheading","text":"Essential Design Patterns"},{"order":9,"type":"paragraph","text":"Once decomposed, services must interact securely and reliably. Key patterns include the API Gateway pattern, Database-per-Service pattern, and the Saga pattern for distributed transactions."},{"order":10,"type":"code","text":"class OrderSaga {
  constructor(orderService, paymentService, inventoryService) {
    this.orderService = orderService;
    this.paymentService = paymentService;
    this.inventoryService = inventoryService;
  }

  async executeOrder(orderData) {
    const order = await this.orderService.createPending(orderData);
    try {
      await this.inventoryService.reserveStock(order.items);
      await this.paymentService.processPayment(order.paymentInfo);
      await this.orderService.completeOrder(order.id);
    } catch (error) {
      await this.orderService.failOrder(order.id);
      await this.inventoryService.releaseStock(order.items);
      throw new Error("Transaction failed. Compensating actions executed.");
    }
  }
}"},{"order":11,"type":"subheading","text":"Conclusion"},{"order":12,"type":"paragraph","text":"Adopting microservices brings organizational scalability and technological flexibility, but introduces distributed systems complexity. Applying proper decomposition and reliability patterns ensures high availability and resilience."}]
